1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,056
An Extreme puzzle begins simply enough.

2
00:00:08,306 --> 00:00:12,562
A few cross-hatching moves reveal a confined group: one and seven.

3
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:14,327
Then comes the fork.

4
00:00:14,577 --> 00:00:16,431
Two positions remain for two.

5
00:00:16,631 --> 00:00:22,007
We choose one—not because we know it's right, but because we can follow where it leads.

6
00:00:22,507 --> 00:00:24,422
One placement forces another.

7
00:00:24,572 --> 00:00:25,920
Box six resolves.

8
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:27,877
Still, no conflict.

9
00:00:28,727 --> 00:00:29,816
The branch holds.

10
00:00:30,816 --> 00:00:33,839
Now two boxes constrain seven in parallel.

11
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:37,574
That fixes another seven, and the puzzle begins to open.

12
00:00:38,124 --> 00:00:39,982
Soon, every cell is filled.

13
00:00:40,882 --> 00:00:42,590
But completion isn't proof.

14
00:00:43,290 --> 00:00:44,771
Return to the assumption.

15
00:00:44,971 --> 00:00:46,685
Trace it through the finished grid.

16
00:00:46,885 --> 00:00:47,981
No duplicate.

17
00:00:48,231 --> 00:00:49,455
No contradiction.

18
00:00:50,305 --> 00:00:51,167
Verified.

19
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:54,145
Sometimes the branch doesn't break.

20
00:00:54,495 --> 00:00:55,966
It leads all the way home.
